# Assembly Maze

![Tests](https://github.com/martinvtt/asm-maze/actions/workflows/tests.yml/badge.svg)

A playable terminal maze written entirely in x86-64 assembly (NASM, Intel syntax,
Linux system calls). No C library, game engine or runtime dependencies.

```text
#####################
#@    #       #    S#
# ### # ##### # ### #
#   #   #   #   #   #
### ##### # ##### # #
#   #     #       # #
# ### ########### # #
#     #       #   # #
# ##### ##### # ### #
#       #           #
#####################
Moves: 0
>
```

## Run

On Ubuntu / WSL running on an x86-64 computer:

```bash
sudo apt install nasm binutils make gdb
git clone https://github.com/martinvtt/asm-maze.git
cd asm-maze
make run
```

Reach `S` with the player `@`. Walls are marked `#`.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Z | Up |
| Q | Left |
| S | Down |
| D | Right |
| R | Restart, including after winning |
| X | Quit |

Type one command and press Enter. Lowercase and uppercase both work. Ctrl+D on an
empty line also quits.

## Features

- One fixed, solvable 21-by-11 maze
- Wall and bounds checks, victory detection, move counter, restart
- Bounded input, EOF handling, interrupted syscall retries
- Debug symbols and a non-executable stack

## Development

```bash
make           # Assemble and link into build/maze
make debug     # Start GDB at the first instruction
make test      # Run the tests (requires Python 3)
make clean     # Remove generated files
```

The tests run the real executable, including two complete games solved by a
breadth-first search on the rendered maze.

